1 SUBROUTINE
    The CALL command runs a subroutine of the same command procedure at a
    new command level, as @ runs a procedure in a file: with parameters
    P1 to P8, local symbols and labels of its own, and an optional output
    file.  The subroutine is the commands between its label's SUBROUTINE
    and its ENDSUBROUTINE.

    Syntax:

        CALL[/OUTPUT=filespec] label [p1 [p2 ... p8]]

        label: SUBROUTINE
            command ...
        ENDSUBROUTINE

    The subroutine ends at its ENDSUBROUTINE or at an EXIT, and CALL's
    status is the subroutine's, as @'s is the procedure's.  A procedure
    run line by line passes over its subroutines: they run only when
    called.  A subroutine inside another subroutine, or inside an IF
    block, can be called only from within it.  A label that isn't found
    is DCL-W-USCALL.  A label not on a SUBROUTINE line runs from there at
    a new level, and CALL then fails with DCL-E-INVCALL.

    Example:

        $ CALL GREET "Hello there"
        $ EXIT
        $ GREET: SUBROUTINE
        $     PRINT P1
        $ ENDSUBROUTINE
